INV-todas (P0). verify-claims returned `confirmed`, exit 0, for a claim naming a
taint label that does not exist, on a fixture that really leaks — os.environ
["API_KEY"] written through open(...).write. The boundary arm, given the same
class of typo in the same command, exits 2 with a did-you-mean. One constraint
vocabulary fails closed and the other fails open.

    source_taint: secret_material          -> confirmed  rc 0    FAILED OPEN
    prohibited_sink_zone: host_filesystem  -> confirmed  rc 0    FAILED OPEN
    boundary: net_sends                    -> Error      rc 2    control
    (correct host_secret / host_fs)        -> violated   rc 1    positive control

MECHANISM, and this project already wrote the argument down. _parse_taint_flow
calls _reject_unknown_keys, so it validates taint_flow KEY NAMES and never their
VALUES. verify_claim then filters findings on `f.taint_label == tf.source_taint
and f.sink_zone == tf.prohibited_sink_zone`; an unrecognised value matches
nothing, the list is empty, and the claim confirms. The boundary arm's guard
carries the identical reasoning in its own comment — "An unknown value here
would otherwise make verify_claim's boundary_map.entries.get return None ->
chain_count 0 -> silent 'confirmed'" — and was applied to one of the two
vocabularies. It is WI-bopoz's unknown-FIELD-name defect one level deeper and
strictly worse: WI-bopoz's symptom was inconclusive, this one was confirmed.

CHEAPEST OF THE FALSE CONFIRMS TO TRIP. A mislabelled overlay takes deliberate
effort; mistyping host_fs, or guessing secret_material for host_secret, is a
first attempt, with the vocabulary printed nowhere on the error path. It now is.

WHY THE CHECK IS NOT BESIDE THE BOUNDARY ONE, which is the only real design
decision here. KNOWN_IO_BOUNDARIES is a constant; these vocabularies are not.
They are assembled from taint_sources/*.yaml, AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP,
AUTO_SINK_ZONE_MAP and any user-supplied --taint-sinks / extra_catalogs — this
suite already contains a fixture declaring a zone (custom_zone) no built-in
mentions, and it is now an explicit non-vacuity guard. So TaintCatalog gains
all_source_labels() / all_sink_zones() reading the RESOLVED catalogue, and the
check runs where the catalogue is resolved. Sanitizer output_taint values are
deliberately excluded: verified in source that they are stored on the sanitizer
record and never assigned to a finding, so admitting them would loosen the
check for values that still cannot match.

DISCLOSED COST: the resolved catalogue is assembled after the behavior map, so a
typo is reported AFTER analysis rather than before. Better than confirming,
worse than failing fast. Hoisting the catalogue load ahead of
_get_or_run_analysis is a separate change and is not folded into a security fix.

FIVE EXISTING TESTS FAILED AND FOUR WERE ASSERTING THE DEFECT. They used
source_taint: "secret" — not a label — as incidental fixture filler while
testing language coverage, data-file handling and the unsupported-language
notice, and asserted rc == 0. With a real label their rc == 0 assertions hold
unchanged, so their subjects were never at issue; the bogus label was.

The fifth is a genuine, intended behaviour change. A CLI --taint-sources
override that displaces a claims-file label used to leave the claim naming that
label `confirmed`, with the recorded rationale "the override displaced it". A
displaced label can be carried by no finding, so that was the tool answering "I
cannot evaluate your claim" with "your claim holds". It exits 2 now, which is
also STRONGER evidence for the precedence that test exists to prove: the error
names the winner and the loser, so the override is visible in the output instead
of inferred from a silence. That test's first assertion — the baseline where
claims_label does seed and the flow is violated — is untouched.

REGRESSION CHECKS. hypergumbo's own self-proof (docs/hypergumbo.claims.yaml,
which declares its own sources, sinks and zone-barrier sanitizer) runs with ZERO
vocabulary errors across all 18 claims, so the resolved-catalogue design accepts
a project's own labels as intended. Separately and not caused by this change,
that self-proof resolves nothing at all — 18 of 18 inconclusive on uncatalogued
bash / csharp / solidity — filed as INV-dabuf rather than left as a surprise.

A mixed claims file is scanned through, not up to the first non-taint claim: the
skip for a boundary claim is a continue, not a break, and a test places a
boundary claim FIRST so a regression to break cannot pass.

